=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported fault — PC1 (10.1.0.100) unable to reach PC2 (10.1.0.101) on the
same VLAN — does not exist in the current network state. Direct verification
shows 0% packet loss and ~1 ms round-trip time between the two hosts, consistent
with pure L2 switching on VLAN 10 with no routing hop involved.

The complete forwarding path was inspected at every layer. Access switch aswa2
(PC1's switch) has eth0 forwarding toward PC1 and eth1 forwarding toward dswa1;
its eth2 (dswa2 uplink) is STP-blocked, which is normal loop prevention in a
dual-uplink access design. Access switch aswa3 (PC2's switch) is in an identical
and healthy state. Distribution switch dswa1 has all three access-facing ports
(eth0, eth1, eth2) and its inter-DSW bond (bond0) in forwarding state, with
VLANs 10, 20, 40, and 99 correctly admitted on every port. The MAC addresses of
both PCs are correctly learned in the FDB tables on both access switches and on
dswa1. There are no ebtables rules on either access switch, and the iptables
FORWARD rules that exist on dswa1 apply only to routed (inter-subnet) traffic
from 10.1.0.0/24 toward 10.3.0.0/24 — a known standing inter-office security
policy that has no effect on bridged intra-VLAN frames. dswa1's OSPF running
configuration references the correct SVI names (br0.10 etc.).

Root cause: the ticket describes a fault that is not present; the Office A
intra-VLAN path between PC1 and PC2 is fully functional.

Corrective action: no remediation is required for the stated symptom. The
helpdesk ticket should be closed as a false report. If the user experienced a
transient outage, it may have self-resolved (e.g., a brief STP topology change);
monitoring can be set up on dswa1's bridge to catch future STP events.
=== END ===
